{{also|frågor}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɹeɪɡə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fragor.wav|Audio (RP)}}

===Etymology 1===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|fragor||a breaking to pieces}}, from {{m|la|frangō||to break}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[loud]] and [[sudden]] [[sound]]; the [[report]] of anything [[burst]]ing; a [[crash]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1709|author={{w|Isaac Watts}}|title=Horae Lyricae
|passage=The direful '''fragor''', when some southern blast / Tears from the Alps a ridge of knotty oaks{{...}}}}

===Etymology 2===
From the above, by confusion with the root of {{m|en|fragrant}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|proscribed}} A strong or sweet [[scent]]; [[fragrance]].
#* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|passage=it seems offensive; and tasted, penetrates a strong brain by its '''fragor'''}}

===References===
{{R:Webster 1913}}

